.9"/><text text-anchor="end" transform="translate(81.921875 178)" fill="#000000" font-size="19.84" font-family="Crimson Text">1.5</text><line xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" x1="93.921875" y1="205.33333333333334" x2="385" y2="205.33333333333334" fill="none" stroke="#000000" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="0.9"/><line xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" x1="87.921875" y1="205.33333333333334" x2="99.921875" y2="205.33333333333334" fill="none" stroke="#000000" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="0.9"/><text text-anchor="end" transform="translate(81.921875 211.33333333333334)" fill="#000000" font-size="19.84" font-family="Crimson Text">1.0</text><line xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" x1="93.921875" y1="238.66666666666669" x2="385" y2="238.66666666666669" fill="none" stroke="#000000" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="0.9"/><line xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" x1="87.921875" y1="238.66666666666669" x2="99.921875" y2="238.66666666666669" fill="none" stroke="#000000" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="0.9"/><text text-anchor="end" transform="translate(81.921875 244.66666666666669)" fill="#000000" font-size="19.84" font-family="Crimson Text">0.5</text><line xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" x1="93.921875" y1="272" x2="385" y2="272" fill="none" stroke="#000000" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="0.9"/><line xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" x1="87.921875" y1="272" x2="99.921875" y2="272" fill="none" stroke="#000000" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="0.9"/><text text-anchor="end" transform="translate(81.921875 278)" fill="#000000" font-size="19.84" font-family="Crimson Text">0</text><text text-anchor="middle" transform="translate(24 172) rotate(-90)" fill="#000000" font-size="19.84" font-family="Crimson Text">Average percentage of </text><text text-anchor="middle" transform="translate(48 172) rotate(-90)" fill="#000000" font-size="19.84" font-family="Crimson Text">nonhexagonal cells by shape</text><line xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" x1="93.921875" y1="72" x2="93.921875" y2="272" fill="none" stroke="#000000" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="0.9"/><rect xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" x="116.3125" y="105.33333333333331" height="166.66666666666669" width="22.390625" fill="#666666" stroke="#000000" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="mitre" stroke-width="0.9"/><rect xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" x="138.703125" y="112" height="160" width="22.390625" fill="#CCCCCC" stroke="#000000" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="mitre" stroke-width="0.9"/><rect xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" x="161.09375" y="272" height="0" width="22.390625" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="mitre" stroke-width="0.9"/><text xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" x="0" y="0" text-anchor="end" transform="translate(159.8184375 291.84) rotate(-40)" fill="#000000" font-size="19.84" font-family="Crimson Text">black dwarf honeybee</text><rect xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" x="205.875" y="98.66666666666666" height="173.33333333333334" width="22.390625" fill="#666666" stroke="#000000" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="mitre" stroke-width="0.9"/><rect xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" x="228.265625" y="131.99999999999997" height="140.00000000000003" width="22.390625" fill="#CCCCCC" stroke="#000000" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="mitre" stroke-width="0.9"/><rect xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" x="250.65625" y="258.6666666666667" height="13.333333333333336" width="22.390625" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="mitre" stroke-width="0.9"/><text xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" x="0" y="0" text-anchor="end" transform="translate(249.3809375 291.84) rotate(-40)" fill="#000000" font-size="19.84" font-family="Crimson Text">dwarf honeybee</text><rect xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" x="295.4375" y="242" height="30.000000000000004" width="22.390625" fill="#666666" stroke="#000000" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="mitre" stroke-width="0.9"/><rect xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" x="317.828125" y="247" height="25" width="22.390625" fill="#CCCCCC" stroke="#000000" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="mitre" stroke-width="0.9"/><rect xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" x="340.21875" y="266.6666666666667" height="5.333333333333334" width="22.390625" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="mitre" stroke-width="0.9"/><text xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" x="0" y="0" text-anchor="end" transform="translate(338.9434375 291.84) rotate(-40)" fill="#000000" font-size="19.84" font-family="Crimson Text">western honeybee</text><text text-anchor="middle" transform="translate(239.4609375 24)" fill="#000000" font-size="19.84" font-family="Crimson Text">Percentage of Nonhexagonal Cells in </text><text text-anchor="middle" transform="translate(239.4609375 48)" fill="#000000" font-size="19.84" font-family="Crimson Text">Hives of Three Honeybee Species</text><text text-anchor="middle" transform="translate(239.4609375 436.0571630859375)" fill="#000000" font-size="19.84" font-family="Crimson Text">Species</text><rect xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" fill="none" x="137.49895477294922" y="453.2171630859375" height="103" width="140.00209045410156" stroke="#000000" stroke-linejoin="mitre" stroke-width="0.9"/><rect xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" x="144.49895477294922" y="465.2171630859375" height="12" width="12" fill="#666666" stroke="#000000" stroke-linejoin="mitre" stroke-width="0.9"/><text transform="translate(166.49895477294922 477.2171630859375)" text-anchor="top" fill="#000000" font-size="19.84" font-family="Crimson Text"> 5-sided cells</text><rect xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" x="144.49895477294922" y="497.2171630859375" height="12" width="12" fill="#CCCCCC" stroke="#000000" stroke-linejoin="mitre" stroke-width="0.9"/><text transform="translate(166.49895477294922 509.2171630859375)" text-anchor="top" fill="#000000" font-size="19.84" font-family="Crimson Text"> 7-sided cells</text><rect xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" x="144.49895477294922" y="529.2171630859375" height="12" width="12" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-linejoin="mitre" stroke-width="0.9"/><text transform="translate(166.49895477294922 541.2171630859375)" text-anchor="top" fill="#000000" font-size="19.84" font-family="Crimson Text"> 8-sided cells</text></g></svg></figure><div class="sr-only" role="region" aria-label="Long description for bar graph titled Percentage of Nonhexagonal Cells in Hives of Three Honeybee Species"><ul>
<li>All values are approximate.</li>
<li>For each data category, the following bars are shown:&nbsp;<br/>
<ul>
<li>5-sided cells</li>
<li>7-sided cells</li>
<li>8-sided cells</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>The average percentage of nonhexagonal cells by shape data for the 3 categories are as follows:<br/>
<ul>
<li>black dwarf honeybee:<br/>
<ul>
<li>5-sided cells: 2.5 percent</li>
<li>7-sided cells: 2.4 percent</li>
<li>8-sided cells: 0.0 percent</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>dwarf honeybee:&nbsp;<br/>
<ul>
<li>5-sided cells: 2.6 percent</li>
<li>7-sided cells: 2.1 percent</li>
<li>8-sided cells: 0.2 percent</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>western honeybee:&nbsp;<br/>
<ul>
<li>5-sided cells: 0.45 percent</li>
<li>7-sided cells: 0.38 percent</li>
<li>8-sided cells: 0.08 percent</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul></div>
<p>Honeybee hives consist mainly of hexagonal (six-sided) units called cells, in which queens lay eggs. Hexagonal cells for eggs that develop into nonreproductive workers are smaller than those for eggs that develop into reproductive drones, though the size difference varies by species. Difference in cell size results in a construction problem&mdash;it&rsquo;s hard to neatly connect sections of small cells to sections of large cells&mdash;that worsens as the difference increases. To fill in gaps between the sections when building a hive, bees rely on cells that have more or fewer than six sides. A student studying beehive structure consults data on three species, concluding that <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the student&rsquo;s conclusion?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4752591e-4937-4f29-9ff2-d4dde7abb1bd","content":"<p>cells for worker eggs are probably closer in size to cells for drone eggs in the hives of the western honeybee than in the hives of the dwarf honeybee and the black dwarf honeybee.</p>"},{"id":"79382478-c20b-4e4b-b54f-2a6aa97f5c6a","content":"<p>both the western honeybee and the black dwarf honeybee probably reserve eight-sided cells for drone eggs, while the dwarf honeybee likely deposits drone eggs in seven-sided cells.</p>"},{"id":"4bba32ec-32e0-4ddc-975a-3a47259930fd","content":"<p>the western honeybee probably relies on many more geometrical shapes when constructing cells than either the dwarf honeybee or the black dwarf honeybee does.</p>"},{"id":"1fe01fff-20cc-4563-9d54-2321693c34d0","content":"<p>the percentage of hexagonal cells is probably slightly lower in the hives of the western honeybee than in the hives of the dwarf honeybee and the black dwarf honeybee.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["4752591e-4937-4f29-9ff2-d4dde7abb1bd"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the student&rsquo;s conclusion about beehive structure. The text explains that in the hives of honeybees, the hexagonal cells housing drone eggs are larger than the hexagonal cells housing worker eggs, and that this size difference results in a construction problem that the bees address by using nonhexagonal cells to fill gaps between sections of drone-egg cells and worker-egg cells. The text also states that the size difference between drone-egg cells and worker-egg cells varies by species of honeybee. The graph displays data on the percentage of nonhexagonal cells in the hives of three species. In the hives of the western honeybee, the percentages of five-sided, seven-sided, and eight-sided cells are all less than 0.5%. But in the hives of the black dwarf honeybee, the percentages of five-sided and seven-sided cells are higher than those for the western honeybee: about 2.5% for both. And for the dwarf honeybee, the percentages of five-sided and seven-sided cells are also higher than those for the western honeybee: slightly over 2.5% and slightly over 2.0%, respectively; additionally, the dwarf honeybee possesses a higher percentage of eight-sided cells than the western honeybee does. Taken altogether, the graph shows that the hives of the western honeybee consist of a smaller percentage of nonhexagonal cells than the hives of the two other species do. Since the nonhexagonal cells exist only to solve the construction problem arising from the difference in size between drone-egg cells and worker-egg cells, a smaller percentage of nonhexagonal cells would be associated with a smaller size difference between the two types of cells. Therefore, it can be concluded from the data that worker-egg cells are probably closer in size to drone-egg cells in the hives of the western honeybee than in the hives of the other two species.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because, as the text states, honeybee species deposit their eggs in hexagonal cells, not in nonhexagonal ones. Thus, the western honeybee and black dwarf honeybee wouldn&rsquo;t deposit drone eggs in eight-sided cells, and the dwarf honeybee wouldn&rsquo;t deposit drone eggs in seven-sided cells. Choice C is incorrect. The text explains that honeybees rely mainly on one geometric shape, the hexagon, when constructing their hives, and the graph shows that the western honeybee relies on the same nonhexagonal shapes as the dwarf honeybee does: five-sided, seven-sided, and eight-sided cells. In other words, the western honeybee and dwarf honeybee rely on the same number of geometric shapes. For the black dwarf honeybee, the graph displays data only for five-sided and seven-sided cells, which suggests a total absence of eight-sided cells. Yet this would be only one less nonhexagonal shape than is seen in the western honeybee. Thus, based on the graph, it would be inaccurate to say that the western honeybee relies on "many more" geometrical shapes than the other two species do. Choice D is incorrect. As the text explains, honeybee hives consist mainly of hexagonal cells, and sections of nonhexagonal cells are used to connect sections of hexagonal cells of different sizes. Since the graph indicates that the percentage of nonhexagonal cells is lower for the western honeybee than it is for the dwarf honeybee or black dwarf honeybee, the western honeybee would conversely have a <em>higher </em>percentage of hexagonal cells than the either the dwarf honeybee or black dwarf honeybee does, not a lower percentage.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"f71802d6","external_id":"30f294ab-b2a2-4e6f-bfc7-f099c4e68738","vaultid":null,"uId":"5fde3f61-5fa4-408e-9ecf-6d7b6ca5a487","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<figure class=\"image\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"400\" height=\"567.2171630859375\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 567.2171630859375\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Bar graph titled Percentage of Nonhexagonal Cells in Hives of Three Honeybee Species. The horizontal axis is labeled Species. 3 data categories are shown. The vertical axis is labeled Average percentage of nonhexagonal cells by shape. It ranges from 0 to 3.0 in increments of 0.5. Refer to long description.\"><g id=\"ed420550-79eb-48d4-af01-cd27cdd08afd\" data-name=\"Layer 1\"><defs>\n      <pattern id=\"bar4\" x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"10\" height=\"100\" patternUnits=\"userSpaceOnUse\" patternTransform=\"rotate(50)\">\n        <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"5\" height=\"100\" fill=\"#CDCDCD\"/>\n        <rect x=\"5\" y=\"0\" width=\"5\" height=\"100\" fill=\"#444444\"/>\n      </pattern>\n    </defs><line xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x1=\"93.921875\" y1=\"72\" x2=\"385\" y2=\"72\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><line xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x1=\"87.921875\" y1=\"72\" x2=\"99.921875\" y2=\"72\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text text-anchor=\"end\" transform=\"translate(81.921875 78)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">3.0</text><line xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x1=\"93.921875\" y1=\"105.33333333333334\" x2=\"385\" y2=\"105.33333333333334\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><line xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x1=\"87.921875\" y1=\"105.33333333333334\" x2=\"99.921875\" y2=\"105.33333333333334\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text text-anchor=\"end\" transform=\"translate(81.921875 111.33333333333334)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">2.5</text><line xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x1=\"93.921875\" y1=\"138.66666666666669\" x2=\"385\" y2=\"138.66666666666669\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><line xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x1=\"87.921875\" y1=\"138.66666666666669\" x2=\"99.921875\" y2=\"138.66666666666669\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text text-anchor=\"end\" transform=\"translate(81.921875 144.66666666666669)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">2.0</text><line xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x1=\"93.921875\" y1=\"172\" x2=\"385\" y2=\"172\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><line xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x1=\"87.921875\" y1=\"172\" x2=\"99.921875\" y2=\"172\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text text-anchor=\"end\" transform=\"translate(81.921875 178)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">1.5</text><line xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x1=\"93.921875\" y1=\"205.33333333333334\" x2=\"385\" y2=\"205.33333333333334\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><line xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x1=\"87.921875\" y1=\"205.33333333333334\" x2=\"99.921875\" y2=\"205.33333333333334\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text text-anchor=\"end\" transform=\"translate(81.921875 211.33333333333334)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">1.0</text><line xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x1=\"93.921875\" y1=\"238.66666666666669\" x2=\"385\" y2=\"238.66666666666669\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><line xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x1=\"87.921875\" y1=\"238.66666666666669\" x2=\"99.921875\" y2=\"238.66666666666669\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text text-anchor=\"end\" transform=\"translate(81.921875 244.66666666666669)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">0.5</text><line xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x1=\"93.921875\" y1=\"272\" x2=\"385\" y2=\"272\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><line xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x1=\"87.921875\" y1=\"272\" x2=\"99.921875\" y2=\"272\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text text-anchor=\"end\" transform=\"translate(81.921875 278)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">0</text><text text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"translate(24 172) rotate(-90)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">Average percentage of </text><text text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"translate(48 172) rotate(-90)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">nonhexagonal cells by shape</text><line xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x1=\"93.921875\" y1=\"72\" x2=\"93.921875\" y2=\"272\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"116.3125\" y=\"105.33333333333331\" height=\"166.66666666666669\" width=\"22.390625\" fill=\"#666666\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"138.703125\" y=\"112\" height=\"160\" width=\"22.390625\" fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"161.09375\" y=\"272\" height=\"0\" width=\"22.390625\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"0\" y=\"0\" text-anchor=\"end\" transform=\"translate(159.8184375 291.84) rotate(-40)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">black dwarf honeybee</text><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"205.875\" y=\"98.66666666666666\" height=\"173.33333333333334\" width=\"22.390625\" fill=\"#666666\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"228.265625\" y=\"131.99999999999997\" height=\"140.00000000000003\" width=\"22.390625\" fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"250.65625\" y=\"258.6666666666667\" height=\"13.333333333333336\" width=\"22.390625\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"0\" y=\"0\" text-anchor=\"end\" transform=\"translate(249.3809375 291.84) rotate(-40)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">dwarf honeybee</text><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"295.4375\" y=\"242\" height=\"30.000000000000004\" width=\"22.390625\" fill=\"#666666\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"317.828125\" y=\"247\" height=\"25\" width=\"22.390625\" fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"340.21875\" y=\"266.6666666666667\" height=\"5.333333333333334\" width=\"22.390625\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"0\" y=\"0\" text-anchor=\"end\" transform=\"translate(338.9434375 291.84) rotate(-40)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">western honeybee</text><text text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"translate(239.4609375 24)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">Percentage of Nonhexagonal Cells in </text><text text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"translate(239.4609375 48)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">Hives of Three Honeybee Species</text><text text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"translate(239.4609375 436.0571630859375)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">Species</text><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" fill=\"none\" x=\"137.49895477294922\" y=\"453.2171630859375\" height=\"103\" width=\"140.00209045410156\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"144.49895477294922\" y=\"465.2171630859375\" height=\"12\" width=\"12\" fill=\"#666666\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text transform=\"translate(166.49895477294922 477.2171630859375)\" text-anchor=\"top\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\"> 5-sided cells</text><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"144.49895477294922\" y=\"497.2171630859375\" height=\"12\" width=\"12\" fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text transform=\"translate(166.49895477294922 509.2171630859375)\" text-anchor=\"top\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\"> 7-sided cells</text><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"144.49895477294922\" y=\"529.2171630859375\" height=\"12\" width=\"12\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text transform=\"translate(166.49895477294922 541.2171630859375)\" text-anchor=\"top\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\"> 8-sided cells</text></g></svg></figure><div class=\"sr-only\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Long description for bar graph titled Percentage of Nonhexagonal Cells in Hives of Three Honeybee Species\"><ul>\n<li>All values are approximate.</li>\n<li>For each data category, the following bars are shown:&nbsp;<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>5-sided cells</li>\n<li>7-sided cells</li>\n<li>8-sided cells</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The average percentage of nonhexagonal cells by shape data for the 3 categories are as follows:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>black dwarf honeybee:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>5-sided cells: 2.5 percent</li>\n<li>7-sided cells: 2.4 percent</li>\n<li>8-sided cells: 0.0 percent</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>dwarf honeybee:&nbsp;<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>5-sided cells: 2.6 percent</li>\n<li>7-sided cells: 2.1 percent</li>\n<li>8-sided cells: 0.2 percent</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>western honeybee:&nbsp;<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>5-sided cells: 0.45 percent</li>\n<li>7-sided cells: 0.38 percent</li>\n<li>8-sided cells: 0.08 percent</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p>Honeybee hives consist mainly of hexagonal (six-sided) units called cells, in which queens lay eggs. Hexagonal cells for eggs that develop into nonreproductive workers are smaller than those for eggs that develop into reproductive drones, though the size difference varies by species. Difference in cell size results in a construction problem&mdash;it&rsquo;s hard to neatly connect sections of small cells to sections of large cells&mdash;that worsens as the difference increases. To fill in gaps between the sections when building a hive, bees rely on cells that have more or fewer than six sides. A student studying beehive structure consults data on three species, concluding that <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the student&rsquo;s conclusion?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4752591e-4937-4f29-9ff2-d4dde7abb1bd","content":"<p>cells for worker eggs are probably closer in size to cells for drone eggs in the hives of the western honeybee than in the hives of the dwarf honeybee and the black dwarf honeybee.</p>"},{"id":"79382478-c20b-4e4b-b54f-2a6aa97f5c6a","content":"<p>both the western honeybee and the black dwarf honeybee probably reserve eight-sided cells for drone eggs, while the dwarf honeybee likely deposits drone eggs in seven-sided cells.</p>"},{"id":"4bba32ec-32e0-4ddc-975a-3a47259930fd","content":"<p>the western honeybee probably relies on many more geometrical shapes when constructing cells than either the dwarf honeybee or the black dwarf honeybee does.</p>"},{"id":"1fe01fff-20cc-4563-9d54-2321693c34d0","content":"<p>the percentage of hexagonal cells is probably slightly lower in the hives of the western honeybee than in the hives of the dwarf honeybee and the black dwarf honeybee.</p>"}],"keys":["4752591e-4937-4f29-9ff2-d4dde7abb1bd"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the student&rsquo;s conclusion about beehive structure. The text explains that in the hives of honeybees, the hexagonal cells housing drone eggs are larger than the hexagonal cells housing worker eggs, and that this size difference results in a construction problem that the bees address by using nonhexagonal cells to fill gaps between sections of drone-egg cells and worker-egg cells. The text also states that the size difference between drone-egg cells and worker-egg cells varies by species of honeybee. The graph displays data on the percentage of nonhexagonal cells in the hives of three species. In the hives of the western honeybee, the percentages of five-sided, seven-sided, and eight-sided cells are all less than 0.5%. But in the hives of the black dwarf honeybee, the percentages of five-sided and seven-sided cells are higher than those for the western honeybee: about 2.5% for both. And for the dwarf honeybee, the percentages of five-sided and seven-sided cells are also higher than those for the western honeybee: slightly over 2.5% and slightly over 2.0%, respectively; additionally, the dwarf honeybee possesses a higher percentage of eight-sided cells than the western honeybee does. Taken altogether, the graph shows that the hives of the western honeybee consist of a smaller percentage of nonhexagonal cells than the hives of the two other species do. Since the nonhexagonal cells exist only to solve the construction problem arising from the difference in size between drone-egg cells and worker-egg cells, a smaller percentage of nonhexagonal cells would be associated with a smaller size difference between the two types of cells. Therefore, it can be concluded from the data that worker-egg cells are probably closer in size to drone-egg cells in the hives of the western honeybee than in the hives of the other two species.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because, as the text states, honeybee species deposit their eggs in hexagonal cells, not in nonhexagonal ones. Thus, the western honeybee and black dwarf honeybee wouldn&rsquo;t deposit drone eggs in eight-sided cells, and the dwarf honeybee wouldn&rsquo;t deposit drone eggs in seven-sided cells. Choice C is incorrect. The text explains that honeybees rely mainly on one geometric shape, the hexagon, when constructing their hives, and the graph shows that the western honeybee relies on the same nonhexagonal shapes as the dwarf honeybee does: five-sided, seven-sided, and eight-sided cells. In other words, the western honeybee and dwarf honeybee rely on the same number of geometric shapes. For the black dwarf honeybee, the graph displays data only for five-sided and seven-sided cells, which suggests a total absence of eight-sided cells. Yet this would be only one less nonhexagonal shape than is seen in the western honeybee. Thus, based on the graph, it would be inaccurate to say that the western honeybee relies on \"many more\" geometrical shapes than the other two species do. Choice D is incorrect. As the text explains, honeybee hives consist mainly of hexagonal cells, and sections of nonhexagonal cells are used to connect sections of hexagonal cells of different sizes. Since the graph indicates that the percentage of nonhexagonal cells is lower for the western honeybee than it is for the dwarf honeybee or black dwarf honeybee, the western honeybee would conversely have a <em>higher </em>percentage of hexagonal cells than the either the dwarf honeybee or black dwarf honeybee does, not a lower percentage.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669205,"updateDate":1743430669205}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669205, 1743430669205),
    ($SATQ$f7bd14de$SATQ$, $SATQ$3bfe8f14-8d16-47a6-ac4c-e7ddb1572611$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$45565ffb-8814-4572-9234-2804436c44ec$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$Central Ideas and Details$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Several scholars have argued that conditions in England in the late ninth through early eleventh centuries&mdash;namely, burgeoning literacy amid running conflicts between England&rsquo;s Anglo-Saxon kingdoms and Danish invaders&mdash;were especially conducive to the production of the Old English epic poem <em>Beowulf</em>, and they have dated the poem&rsquo;s composition accordingly. It is not inconceivable that <em>Beowulf</em> emerged from such a context, but privileging contextual fit over the linguistic evidence of an eighth- or even seventh-century composition requires a level of justification that thus far has not been presented.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main idea of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"ad10818e-2f6d-4fff-9260-9d36c03f3488","content":"<p>Although there are some grounds for believing that <em>Beowulf</em> was composed between the late ninth and early eleventh centuries, advocates for that view tend to rely on evidence that has been called into question by advocates for an earlier date.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"8c05bd51-dc69-4382-9510-0320fe069b7a","content":"<p>Although several scholars have dated <em>Beowulf</em> to the late ninth through early eleventh centuries, others have argued that doing so privileges a controversial interpretation of the social conditions of the period.</p>"},{"id":"35b1e5e2-4982-461f-a7fd-62bc1a354b30","content":"<p>Although <em>Beowulf</em> fits well with the historical context of England in the late ninth through early eleventh centuries, it fits equally well with the historical context of England in the seventh and eighth centuries.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"59079944-9819-44e6-8bae-b9f5e0840814","content":"<p>Although the claim of a late ninth- through early eleventh-century composition date for <em>Beowulf</em> has some plausibility, advocates for the claim have not compellingly addressed evidence suggesting an earlier date.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["59079944-9819-44e6-8bae-b9f5e0840814"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most accurately states the main idea of the text. The text states that some scholars have dated the composition of <em>Beowulf</em> to the late ninth through early eleventh centuries due to the poem&rsquo;s fit with that period&rsquo;s historical context. The text goes on to say that while it is &ldquo;not inconceivable that <em>Beowulf</em> emerged from such a context&rdquo;&mdash;that is, it is possible that <em>Beowulf</em> was composed during the late ninth through eleventh centuries&mdash;there is linguistic evidence that the poem was composed earlier, in the seventh or eighth century. According to the text, favoring the historical context over the linguistic evidence requires justification that scholars have not yet supplied. In other words, the text suggests that scholars who favor the later composition date need to explain why the poem&rsquo;s fit with historical context should take precedence over the linguistic evidence, but they have not yet done so. Thus, the main idea of the text is that while there is some plausibility to the later composition date, advocates for the later date have not compellingly addressed evidence suggestive of an earlier date.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text says that scholars who date the poem to the late ninth through early eleventh centuries have failed to account for the linguistic evidence that the poem may have been composed earlier, not that the evidence those scholars cite in favor of their view is unreliable or that anyone has cast doubt on that evidence. In other words, the text does not suggest that there are problems with the evidence cited by advocates of the later composition date, only that there is other evidence of an earlier composition date that those advocates need to consider.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect because nothing in the text suggests that those scholars who date the poem to the late ninth through early eleventh centuries are giving priority to a controversial view of the social conditions at that time. The text makes no reference to any controversy about how scholars interpret that historical period. Instead, the text suggests that scholars who date the poem on the basis of its fit with the historical context of England in the late ninth through early eleventh centuries have failed to account for linguistic evidence that the poem may have been composed earlier.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because the text says nothing about how well the poem fits the historical context of England in the seventh and eighth centuries, let alone that it fits that historical context as well as it fits the historical context of the late ninth through early eleventh centuries. Rather, the text says that there is linguistic evidence that the poem may have been composed in the seventh or eighth century.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-042-CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$7227065f-14e0-4bbf-b080-c7dcf1b56d9a$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"f7bd14de","external_id":"3bfe8f14-8d16-47a6-ac4c-e7ddb1572611","vaultid":"45565ffb-8814-4572-9234-2804436c44ec","uId":"3f3312e7-121f-46a0-8b56-b35fe07f3abe","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"CID","skill_desc":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Several scholars have argued that conditions in England in the late ninth through early eleventh centuries&mdash;namely, burgeoning literacy amid running conflicts between England&rsquo;s Anglo-Saxon kingdoms and Danish invaders&mdash;were especially conducive to the production of the Old English epic poem <em>Beowulf</em>, and they have dated the poem&rsquo;s composition accordingly. It is not inconceivable that <em>Beowulf</em> emerged from such a context, but privileging contextual fit over the linguistic evidence of an eighth- or even seventh-century composition requires a level of justification that thus far has not been presented.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main idea of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"ad10818e-2f6d-4fff-9260-9d36c03f3488","content":"<p>Although there are some grounds for believing that <em>Beowulf</em> was composed between the late ninth and early eleventh centuries, advocates for that view tend to rely on evidence that has been called into question by advocates for an earlier date.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"8c05bd51-dc69-4382-9510-0320fe069b7a","content":"<p>Although several scholars have dated <em>Beowulf</em> to the late ninth through early eleventh centuries, others have argued that doing so privileges a controversial interpretation of the social conditions of the period.</p>"},{"id":"35b1e5e2-4982-461f-a7fd-62bc1a354b30","content":"<p>Although <em>Beowulf</em> fits well with the historical context of England in the late ninth through early eleventh centuries, it fits equally well with the historical context of England in the seventh and eighth centuries.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"59079944-9819-44e6-8bae-b9f5e0840814","content":"<p>Although the claim of a late ninth- through early eleventh-century composition date for <em>Beowulf</em> has some plausibility, advocates for the claim have not compellingly addressed evidence suggesting an earlier date.</p>"}],"keys":["59079944-9819-44e6-8bae-b9f5e0840814"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most accurately states the main idea of the text. The text states that some scholars have dated the composition of <em>Beowulf</em> to the late ninth through early eleventh centuries due to the poem&rsquo;s fit with that period&rsquo;s historical context. The text goes on to say that while it is &ldquo;not inconceivable that <em>Beowulf</em> emerged from such a context&rdquo;&mdash;that is, it is possible that <em>Beowulf</em> was composed during the late ninth through eleventh centuries&mdash;there is linguistic evidence that the poem was composed earlier, in the seventh or eighth century. According to the text, favoring the historical context over the linguistic evidence requires justification that scholars have not yet supplied. In other words, the text suggests that scholars who favor the later composition date need to explain why the poem&rsquo;s fit with historical context should take precedence over the linguistic evidence, but they have not yet done so. Thus, the main idea of the text is that while there is some plausibility to the later composition date, advocates for the later date have not compellingly addressed evidence suggestive of an earlier date.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text says that scholars who date the poem to the late ninth through early eleventh centuries have failed to account for the linguistic evidence that the poem may have been composed earlier, not that the evidence those scholars cite in favor of their view is unreliable or that anyone has cast doubt on that evidence. In other words, the text does not suggest that there are problems with the evidence cited by advocates of the later composition date, only that there is other evidence of an earlier composition date that those advocates need to consider.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect because nothing in the text suggests that those scholars who date the poem to the late ninth through early eleventh centuries are giving priority to a controversial view of the social conditions at that time. The text makes no reference to any controversy about how scholars interpret that historical period. Instead, the text suggests that scholars who date the poem on the basis of its fit with the historical context of England in the late ninth through early eleventh centuries have failed to account for linguistic evidence that the poem may have been composed earlier.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because the text says nothing about how well the poem fits the historical context of England in the seventh and eighth centuries, let alone that it fits that historical context as well as it fits the historical context of the late ninth through early eleventh centuries. Rather, the text says that there is linguistic evidence that the poem may have been composed in the seventh or eighth century.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-042-CID","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"7227065f-14e0-4bbf-b080-c7dcf1b56d9a","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959322,"updateDate":1691007959322}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959322, 1691007959322),
    ($SATQ$f8244f7c$SATQ$, $SATQ$58d50e12-1f6b-47db-a28d-eea68a044b02$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><figure class="table"><table class="gdr"><caption style="caption-side: top;"><p style="text-align: center;">North American Thrasher Mean Bill Size and Habitat Temperature Range</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Species</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Mean bill surface area (<span aria-hidden="true">cm<sup>2</sup></span><span class="sr-only">cm squared</span>)</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Mean maximum temperature of warmest month (&deg;C)</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;">Mean minimum temperature of coldest month (&deg;C)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Brown thrasher</th><td style="text-align: center;">1.86</td><td style="text-align: center;">30.40</td><td style="text-align: center;">&minus;4.29</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Bendire&rsquo;s thrasher</th><td style="text-align: center;">1.98</td><td style="text-align: center;">36.57</td><td style="text-align: center;">0.24</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Long-billed thrasher</th><td style="text-align: center;">2.24</td><td style="text-align: center;">35.27</td><td style="text-align: center;">8.82</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Cozumel thrasher</th><td style="text-align: center;">2.28</td><td style="text-align: center;">33.27</td><td style="text-align: center;">18.21</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" style="text-align: left;">Ocellated thrasher</th><td style="text-align: center;">3.26</td><td style="text-align: center;">27.56</td><td style="text-align: center;">5.45</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>
<p>It has been hypothesized that since birds can dissipate excess heat through their bills, bill size should increase with habitat temperature. To evaluate this hypothesis for a 2021 study, Charlotte Probst and colleagues gathered data on mean bill surface area of species of North American thrashers (genus: <em>Toxostoma</em>) as well as on climate conditions of the birds&rsquo; native habitats. Based on their data, Probst and colleagues concluded that the hypothesis was not fully supported.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support Probst and colleagues&rsquo; conclusion?&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b41ce5c4-6afc-49d5-acbb-b5fc181c6063","content":"<p>Although the Bendire&rsquo;s thrasher has one of the smallest mean bill surface areas of the birds included in the table, its habitat has one of the lowest mean maximum temperatures in the warmest month and one of the lowest mean minimum temperatures in the coldest month.</p>"},{"id":"0d5d2d15-907a-4933-9688-f9f4cd632912","content":"<p>Although the Cozumel thrasher has the second greatest mean bill surface area of the birds included in the table, its habitat&rsquo;s mean temperature in the warmest month is significantly higher than that of the other birds&rsquo; habitats.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"aa6dee9d-c591-4a14-ace1-9b008bd3fec4","content":"<p>Of the birds included in the table, the brown thrasher has the smallest mean bill surface area and the habitat with the lowest mean minimum temperature in the coldest month, while the long-billed thrasher has the second-largest mean bill surface area and the habitat with the second-highest mean minimum temperature in the coldest month.</p>"},{"id":"15b9d6fe-f825-44e3-be39-ab4e7ca4bd5f","content":"<p>Of the birds included in the table, the ocellated thrasher has the largest mean bill surface area and the habitat with the lowest mean maximum temperature in the warmest month, while the Bendire&rsquo;s thrasher has the second smallest mean bill surface area and the habitat with the highest mean maximum temperature in the warmest month.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["15b9d6fe-f825-44e3-be39-ab4e7ca4bd5f"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most effectively uses data from the table to support Probst and colleagues&rsquo; conclusion about the hypothesis regarding the relationship between birds&rsquo; bill size and habitat temperature. The text states that although it has been hypothesized that birds&rsquo; bill size should increase with habitat temperature (an adaptation that reflects the fact that birds can release excess heat through their bills), Probst and colleagues concluded after studying mean bill surface area in North American thrashers that this hypothesis was not fully supported. In other words, Probst and colleagues did not observe the predicted positive relationship between mean bill surface area and habitat temperature in the thrashers they studied. The data in the table about the ocellated thrasher support Probst and colleagues&rsquo; conclusion because this species has the greatest mean bill surface area (3.26 <span aria-hidden="true">cm<sup>2</sup></span><span class="sr-only">cm squared</span>), but its habitat has the lowest mean maximum temperature in the warmest month (27.56&deg;C). If the hypothesis were fully supported, species such as the ocellated thrasher that have colder habitats would likely also have smaller bills. Similarly, the data about the Bendire&rsquo;s thrasher support Probst and colleagues&rsquo; conclusion because this species has the second-smallest mean bill surface area (1.98 <span aria-hidden="true">cm<sup>2</sup></span><span class="sr-only">cm squared</span>), yet its habitat has the highest mean maximum temperature in the warmest month (36.57&deg;C). If the hypothesis were fully supported, the Bendire&rsquo;s thrasher and other species with warmer habitats would also have larger bills. Because data in the table show that the ocellated thrasher and the Bendire&rsquo;s thrasher have the opposite or near opposite of the hypothesized relationship between bill size and habitat temperature, these examples support Probst and colleagues&rsquo; conclusion that this hypothesis isn&rsquo;t fully supported.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it misrepresents the data in the table. Although the Bendire&rsquo;s thrasher has the second-smallest mean bill surface area of the species that are included in the table and although its habitat has the second-lowest mean minimum temperature in the coldest month, this habitat has the highest (rather than the lowest) mean maximum temperature in the warmest month. Moreover, the data about this species&rsquo; bill surface area and mean minimum temperature suggest that smaller bills are associated with colder habitats, supporting rather than challenging the hypothesis that bill size increases with habitat temperature. Choice B is incorrect because it misrepresents the data in the table. Although the Cozumel thrasher has the second-greatest mean bill surface area of the species that are included in the table, its habitat&rsquo;s mean maximum temperature in the warmest month is not "significantly higher" than the mean maximum temperatures of the other habitats. On the contrary, the mean maximum temperature of the Cozumel thrasher&rsquo;s habitat is lower than that observed for the habitats of both the Bendire&rsquo;s thrasher and the long-billed thrasher. Choice C is incorrect because it misrepresents the data in the table. Although this choice accurately describes the brown thrasher&rsquo;s mean bill surface area and habitat temperature, it incorrectly states that the long-billed thrasher has the second-largest (rather than the third-largest) mean bill surface area of the species included. In addition, instead of revealing a lack of support for the hypothesis that bill size increases with habitat temperature, the data in this choice broadly reflect the hypothesized relationship.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"f8244f7c","external_id":"58d50e12-1f6b-47db-a28d-eea68a044b02","vaultid":null,"uId":"3ab7a7b1-1668-4fbe-bad4-4ed573753ae3","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><figure class=\"table\"><table class=\"gdr\"><caption style=\"caption-side: top;\"><p style=\"text-align: center;\">North American Thrasher Mean Bill Size and Habitat Temperature Range</p></caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Species</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Mean bill surface area (<span aria-hidden=\"true\">cm<sup>2</sup></span><span class=\"sr-only\">cm squared</span>)</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Mean maximum temperature of warmest month (&deg;C)</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-align: center;vertical-align: bottom;\">Mean minimum temperature of coldest month (&deg;C)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Brown thrasher</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">1.86</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">30.40</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">&minus;4.29</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Bendire&rsquo;s thrasher</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">1.98</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">36.57</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">0.24</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Long-billed thrasher</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">2.24</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">35.27</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">8.82</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Cozumel thrasher</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">2.28</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">33.27</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">18.21</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Ocellated thrasher</th><td style=\"text-align: center;\">3.26</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">27.56</td><td style=\"text-align: center;\">5.45</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></p>\n<p>It has been hypothesized that since birds can dissipate excess heat through their bills, bill size should increase with habitat temperature. To evaluate this hypothesis for a 2021 study, Charlotte Probst and colleagues gathered data on mean bill surface area of species of North American thrashers (genus: <em>Toxostoma</em>) as well as on climate conditions of the birds&rsquo; native habitats. Based on their data, Probst and colleagues concluded that the hypothesis was not fully supported.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support Probst and colleagues&rsquo; conclusion?&nbsp;</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"b41ce5c4-6afc-49d5-acbb-b5fc181c6063","content":"<p>Although the Bendire&rsquo;s thrasher has one of the smallest mean bill surface areas of the birds included in the table, its habitat has one of the lowest mean maximum temperatures in the warmest month and one of the lowest mean minimum temperatures in the coldest month.</p>"},{"id":"0d5d2d15-907a-4933-9688-f9f4cd632912","content":"<p>Although the Cozumel thrasher has the second greatest mean bill surface area of the birds included in the table, its habitat&rsquo;s mean temperature in the warmest month is significantly higher than that of the other birds&rsquo; habitats.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"aa6dee9d-c591-4a14-ace1-9b008bd3fec4","content":"<p>Of the birds included in the table, the brown thrasher has the smallest mean bill surface area and the habitat with the lowest mean minimum temperature in the coldest month, while the long-billed thrasher has the second-largest mean bill surface area and the habitat with the second-highest mean minimum temperature in the coldest month.</p>"},{"id":"15b9d6fe-f825-44e3-be39-ab4e7ca4bd5f","content":"<p>Of the birds included in the table, the ocellated thrasher has the largest mean bill surface area and the habitat with the lowest mean maximum temperature in the warmest month, while the Bendire&rsquo;s thrasher has the second smallest mean bill surface area and the habitat with the highest mean maximum temperature in the warmest month.</p>"}],"keys":["15b9d6fe-f825-44e3-be39-ab4e7ca4bd5f"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most effectively uses data from the table to support Probst and colleagues&rsquo; conclusion about the hypothesis regarding the relationship between birds&rsquo; bill size and habitat temperature. The text states that although it has been hypothesized that birds&rsquo; bill size should increase with habitat temperature (an adaptation that reflects the fact that birds can release excess heat through their bills), Probst and colleagues concluded after studying mean bill surface area in North American thrashers that this hypothesis was not fully supported. In other words, Probst and colleagues did not observe the predicted positive relationship between mean bill surface area and habitat temperature in the thrashers they studied. The data in the table about the ocellated thrasher support Probst and colleagues&rsquo; conclusion because this species has the greatest mean bill surface area (3.26 <span aria-hidden=\"true\">cm<sup>2</sup></span><span class=\"sr-only\">cm squared</span>), but its habitat has the lowest mean maximum temperature in the warmest month (27.56&deg;C). If the hypothesis were fully supported, species such as the ocellated thrasher that have colder habitats would likely also have smaller bills. Similarly, the data about the Bendire&rsquo;s thrasher support Probst and colleagues&rsquo; conclusion because this species has the second-smallest mean bill surface area (1.98 <span aria-hidden=\"true\">cm<sup>2</sup></span><span class=\"sr-only\">cm squared</span>), yet its habitat has the highest mean maximum temperature in the warmest month (36.57&deg;C). If the hypothesis were fully supported, the Bendire&rsquo;s thrasher and other species with warmer habitats would also have larger bills. Because data in the table show that the ocellated thrasher and the Bendire&rsquo;s thrasher have the opposite or near opposite of the hypothesized relationship between bill size and habitat temperature, these examples support Probst and colleagues&rsquo; conclusion that this hypothesis isn&rsquo;t fully supported.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it misrepresents the data in the table. Although the Bendire&rsquo;s thrasher has the second-smallest mean bill surface area of the species that are included in the table and although its habitat has the second-lowest mean minimum temperature in the coldest month, this habitat has the highest (rather than the lowest) mean maximum temperature in the warmest month. Moreover, the data about this species&rsquo; bill surface area and mean minimum temperature suggest that smaller bills are associated with colder habitats, supporting rather than challenging the hypothesis that bill size increases with habitat temperature. Choice B is incorrect because it misrepresents the data in the table. Although the Cozumel thrasher has the second-greatest mean bill surface area of the species that are included in the table, its habitat&rsquo;s mean maximum temperature in the warmest month is not \"significantly higher\" than the mean maximum temperatures of the other habitats. On the contrary, the mean maximum temperature of the Cozumel thrasher&rsquo;s habitat is lower than that observed for the habitats of both the Bendire&rsquo;s thrasher and the long-billed thrasher. Choice C is incorrect because it misrepresents the data in the table. Although this choice accurately describes the brown thrasher&rsquo;s mean bill surface area and habitat temperature, it incorrectly states that the long-billed thrasher has the second-largest (rather than the third-largest) mean bill surface area of the species included. In addition, instead of revealing a lack of support for the hypothesis that bill size increases with habitat temperature, the data in this choice broadly reflect the hypothesized relationship.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061177,"updateDate":1755117061177}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061177, 1755117061177),
    ($SATQ$f8befe75$SATQ$, $SATQ$dbf413aa-7ed0-4c04-a674-6de10190f3f5$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$72bcc16d-01e6-45de-ae37-a8925fbfefe2$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$Central Ideas and Details$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Many intellectual histories of the Black Power movement of the 1960s and 1970s rely heavily on essays and other explicitly ideological works as primary sources, a tendency that can overrepresent the perspectives of a small number of thinkers, most of whom were male. Historian Ashley D. Farmer has shown that expanding the array of primary sources to encompass more types of print material&mdash;including political cartoons, advertisements, and artwork&mdash;leads to a much better understanding of the movement and the crucial and diverse roles that Black women played in shaping it.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the main idea of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"97e44d0c-a4eb-456a-b542-ca3fbcfd337f","content":"<p>Farmer&rsquo;s methods and research have enriched the historical understanding of the Black Power movement and Black women&rsquo;s contributions to it.</p>"},{"id":"b9c851a7-63c1-416e-8254-1e6a8b1ca650","content":"<p>Before Farmer&rsquo;s research, historians had largely ignored the intellectual dimensions of the Black Power movement.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"2cb34289-f326-4c3f-ba05-4e585e2b97c4","content":"<p>Other historians of the Black Power movement have criticized Farmer&rsquo;s use of unconventional primary sources.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"188419a3-27f6-4f71-9e0d-05e6b45b89b1","content":"<p>The figures in the Black Power movement whom historians tend to cite would have agreed with Farmer&rsquo;s conclusions about women&rsquo;s roles in the movement.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["97e44d0c-a4eb-456a-b542-ca3fbcfd337f"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. It best describes the main idea of the text. The text starts by saying that historians of the Black Power movement are too reliant on openly ideological works, which were written mostly by men, as sources. The text then describes Farmer&rsquo;s research: she has shown that including other kinds of sources leads to a better understanding of the Black Power movement and the role Black women played in it.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t describe the main idea of the text. In fact, it conflicts with the text. The text says that historians have relied on "essays and other explicitly ideological works," which suggests that they <em>have</em> studied at least some of the intellectual dimensions of the Black Power movement. Choice C is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t describe the main idea of the text. The text never mentions how other historians of the Black Power movement view Farmer&rsquo;s use of unconventional sources. In fact, the text itself argues in favor of Farmer&rsquo;s research, claiming that it has led to a "much better understanding of the movement." Choice D is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t describe the main idea of the text. The text never mentions what any figures in the Black Power movement thought about women&rsquo;s roles in the movement.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-001-CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$4794d87e-e72f-4a11-b9e3-eaa166d47668$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"f8befe75","external_id":"dbf413aa-7ed0-4c04-a674-6de10190f3f5","vaultid":"72bcc16d-01e6-45de-ae37-a8925fbfefe2","uId":"8880cab1-33c5-4a3e-a46e-4cce6b82a521","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"CID","skill_desc":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Many intellectual histories of the Black Power movement of the 1960s and 1970s rely heavily on essays and other explicitly ideological works as primary sources, a tendency that can overrepresent the perspectives of a small number of thinkers, most of whom were male. Historian Ashley D. Farmer has shown that expanding the array of primary sources to encompass more types of print material&mdash;including political cartoons, advertisements, and artwork&mdash;leads to a much better understanding of the movement and the crucial and diverse roles that Black women played in shaping it.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the main idea of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"97e44d0c-a4eb-456a-b542-ca3fbcfd337f","content":"<p>Farmer&rsquo;s methods and research have enriched the historical understanding of the Black Power movement and Black women&rsquo;s contributions to it.</p>"},{"id":"b9c851a7-63c1-416e-8254-1e6a8b1ca650","content":"<p>Before Farmer&rsquo;s research, historians had largely ignored the intellectual dimensions of the Black Power movement.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"2cb34289-f326-4c3f-ba05-4e585e2b97c4","content":"<p>Other historians of the Black Power movement have criticized Farmer&rsquo;s use of unconventional primary sources.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"188419a3-27f6-4f71-9e0d-05e6b45b89b1","content":"<p>The figures in the Black Power movement whom historians tend to cite would have agreed with Farmer&rsquo;s conclusions about women&rsquo;s roles in the movement.</p>"}],"keys":["97e44d0c-a4eb-456a-b542-ca3fbcfd337f"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. It best describes the main idea of the text. The text starts by saying that historians of the Black Power movement are too reliant on openly ideological works, which were written mostly by men, as sources. The text then describes Farmer&rsquo;s research: she has shown that including other kinds of sources leads to a better understanding of the Black Power movement and the role Black women played in it.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t describe the main idea of the text. In fact, it conflicts with the text. The text says that historians have relied on \"essays and other explicitly ideological works,\" which suggests that they <em>have</em> studied at least some of the intellectual dimensions of the Black Power movement. Choice C is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t describe the main idea of the text. The text never mentions how other historians of the Black Power movement view Farmer&rsquo;s use of unconventional sources. In fact, the text itself argues in favor of Farmer&rsquo;s research, claiming that it has led to a \"much better understanding of the movement.\" Choice D is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t describe the main idea of the text. The text never mentions what any figures in the Black Power movement thought about women&rsquo;s roles in the movement.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-001-CID","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"4794d87e-e72f-4a11-b9e3-eaa166d47668","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959310,"updateDate":1691007959310}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959310, 1691007959310),
    ($SATQ$f942646f$SATQ$, $SATQ$11018df2-968a-4ff7-ba6c-537ca7649b01$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$51c76a6c-42a4-465b-b6cc-56e3e842b455$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$INF$SATQ$, $SATQ$Inferences$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Researchers Suchithra Rajendran and Maximilian Popfinger modeled varying levels of passenger redistribution from short-haul flights (flights of 50 to 210 minutes, from takeoff to landing) to high-speed rail trips. Planes travel faster than trains, but air travel typically requires 3 hours of lead time for security, baggage handling, and boarding that rail travel doesn&rsquo;t, so short-haul routes take similar amounts of time by air and by rail. However, the model suggests that as rail passenger volumes approach current capacity limits, long lead times emerge. Therefore, for rail to remain a viable alternative to short-haul flights, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"931558d4-5c96-4ae0-b318-29aae6a45e8c","content":"<p>rail systems should offer fewer long-haul routes and airlines should offer more long-haul routes.</p>"},{"id":"c21e11c2-8b14-4d14-b95b-6faf0d427e2b","content":"<p>rail systems may need to schedule additional trains for these routes.</p>"},{"id":"53066023-84cb-4936-a547-b17eb8f05aa2","content":"<p>security, baggage handling, and boarding procedures used by airlines may need to be implemented for rail systems.</p>"},{"id":"e12a5f5d-4698-42a7-bd52-543261496203","content":"<p>passengers who travel by rail for these routes will need to accept that lead times will be similar to those for air travel.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["c21e11c2-8b14-4d14-b95b-6faf0d427e2b"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. Air travel usually requires much more &ldquo;lead time&rdquo; than train travel, so short flights end up taking the same amount of time as a train trip to the same destination. But train travel starts to need more &ldquo;lead time&rdquo; when the trains approach their capacity limits. This suggests that train companies should add more trains for these routes if they want to encourage travelers to take a train instead of a plane. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This inference isn&rsquo;t supported. The text never discusses &ldquo;long-haul routes&rdquo; for either air travel or rail travel, so there is no basis to make this inference. Choice C is incorrect. This inference isn&rsquo;t supported. The text only mentions these procedures to explain why the &ldquo;lead time&rdquo; is so long for air travel. It never suggests that trains need to start implementing these procedures too. Choice D is incorrect. This inference isn&rsquo;t supported. The goal is to make sure that trains &ldquo;remain a viable alternative&rdquo; to short flights, which suggests that anything that makes train travel take longer should be avoided. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-INF-H003$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$3ffca82d-b080-41eb-a12d-75ce74e9f416$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"f942646f","external_id":"11018df2-968a-4ff7-ba6c-537ca7649b01","vaultid":"51c76a6c-42a4-465b-b6cc-56e3e842b455","uId":"88b9aa5f-19b1-4088-a3c9-eacbe273d519","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"INF","skill_desc":"Inferences","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Researchers Suchithra Rajendran and Maximilian Popfinger modeled varying levels of passenger redistribution from short-haul flights (flights of 50 to 210 minutes, from takeoff to landing) to high-speed rail trips. Planes travel faster than trains, but air travel typically requires 3 hours of lead time for security, baggage handling, and boarding that rail travel doesn&rsquo;t, so short-haul routes take similar amounts of time by air and by rail. However, the model suggests that as rail passenger volumes approach current capacity limits, long lead times emerge. Therefore, for rail to remain a viable alternative to short-haul flights, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"931558d4-5c96-4ae0-b318-29aae6a45e8c","content":"<p>rail systems should offer fewer long-haul routes and airlines should offer more long-haul routes.</p>"},{"id":"c21e11c2-8b14-4d14-b95b-6faf0d427e2b","content":"<p>rail systems may need to schedule additional trains for these routes.</p>"},{"id":"53066023-84cb-4936-a547-b17eb8f05aa2","content":"<p>security, baggage handling, and boarding procedures used by airlines may need to be implemented for rail systems.</p>"},{"id":"e12a5f5d-4698-42a7-bd52-543261496203","content":"<p>passengers who travel by rail for these routes will need to accept that lead times will be similar to those for air travel.</p>"}],"keys":["c21e11c2-8b14-4d14-b95b-6faf0d427e2b"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. Air travel usually requires much more &ldquo;lead time&rdquo; than train travel, so short flights end up taking the same amount of time as a train trip to the same destination. But train travel starts to need more &ldquo;lead time&rdquo; when the trains approach their capacity limits. This suggests that train companies should add more trains for these routes if they want to encourage travelers to take a train instead of a plane. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This inference isn&rsquo;t supported. The text never discusses &ldquo;long-haul routes&rdquo; for either air travel or rail travel, so there is no basis to make this inference. Choice C is incorrect. This inference isn&rsquo;t supported. The text only mentions these procedures to explain why the &ldquo;lead time&rdquo; is so long for air travel. It never suggests that trains need to start implementing these procedures too. Choice D is incorrect. This inference isn&rsquo;t supported. The goal is to make sure that trains &ldquo;remain a viable alternative&rdquo; to short flights, which suggests that anything that makes train travel take longer should be avoided. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-INF-H003","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"3ffca82d-b080-41eb-a12d-75ce74e9f416","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959368,"updateDate":1691007959368}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959368, 1691007959368),
    ($SATQ$f9bd4e61$SATQ$, $SATQ$74524b04-95ed-45c5-be93-30cc8f3484d1$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$32574653-d830-44c4-9f65-2ffa5fa5d73e$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$INF$SATQ$, $SATQ$Inferences$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>German theater practitioner Bertolt Brecht (1898&ndash;1956) believed that theater should elicit an intellectual rather than an emotional response from audiences, provoking them to consider social and political realities that extend beyond the characters and events depicted onstage. Brecht&rsquo;s influence can be seen in English playwright Caryl Churchill&rsquo;s 1979 play <em>Cloud 9</em>: although the play sometimes invites empathetic reactions, it primarily works to engage audiences in an interrogation of patriarchy and colonialism, which it does by placing audiences at a distance, thereby encouraging them to <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"6c609147-ac70-4040-af22-8942e9a260e5","content":"<p>focus on the characters&rsquo; beliefs about social and political issues as revealed by the characters&rsquo; actions.</p>"},{"id":"7becf174-60d9-4237-a1da-5e210f46a559","content":"<p>reflect on social and political phenomena not directly related to patriarchy and colonialism.</p>"},{"id":"d9bf10b6-130b-4dba-a698-1eb8dbd7e76a","content":"<p>recognize pertinent social and political parallels between Germany during Brecht&rsquo;s time and England at the time when Churchill was writing <em>Cloud 9</em>.</p>"},{"id":"0a194dbc-567d-4f26-944a-345899d4fb42","content":"<p>be dispassionate as they think critically about the social and political questions raised by the play.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["0a194dbc-567d-4f26-944a-345899d4fb42"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. Churchill&rsquo;s play was influenced by Brecht&rsquo;s belief that theater should elicit an intellectual rather than an emotional response from audiences, making them think about social and political issues that also exist outside of the play. Therefore, it makes sense that Churchill would strive to have audiences think dispassionately (i.e., without emotion) and critically about the social and political questions raised by the play. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The passage mentions that Churchill was influenced by Brecht&rsquo;s belief that plays should provoke audience members &ldquo;to consider social and political realities that extend beyond the characters and events depicted onstage,&rdquo; so there&rsquo;s no basis for an inference about audience members deeply engaging with characters&rsquo; beliefs and actions. Choice B is incorrect. Reflecting on things that aren&rsquo;t related to patriarchy and colonialism wouldn&rsquo;t &ldquo;engage audiences in an interrogation of patriarchy and colonialism.&rdquo; That creates a confusing contradiction. Choice C is incorrect. No mention is made of social and political issues in Germany during Brecht&rsquo;s time, so there&rsquo;s no basis for this inference. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-INF-H005$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$b61833f1-23b8-4397-bcc1-aed2334b4260$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"f9bd4e61","external_id":"74524b04-95ed-45c5-be93-30cc8f3484d1","vaultid":"32574653-d830-44c4-9f65-2ffa5fa5d73e","uId":"3d20bcf2-f90a-4fb5-a722-1926accd57dd","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"INF","skill_desc":"Inferences","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>German theater practitioner Bertolt Brecht (1898&ndash;1956) believed that theater should elicit an intellectual rather than an emotional response from audiences, provoking them to consider social and political realities that extend beyond the characters and events depicted onstage. Brecht&rsquo;s influence can be seen in English playwright Caryl Churchill&rsquo;s 1979 play <em>Cloud 9</em>: although the play sometimes invites empathetic reactions, it primarily works to engage audiences in an interrogation of patriarchy and colonialism, which it does by placing audiences at a distance, thereby encouraging them to <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"6c609147-ac70-4040-af22-8942e9a260e5","content":"<p>focus on the characters&rsquo; beliefs about social and political issues as revealed by the characters&rsquo; actions.</p>"},{"id":"7becf174-60d9-4237-a1da-5e210f46a559","content":"<p>reflect on social and political phenomena not directly related to patriarchy and colonialism.</p>"},{"id":"d9bf10b6-130b-4dba-a698-1eb8dbd7e76a","content":"<p>recognize pertinent social and political parallels between Germany during Brecht&rsquo;s time and England at the time when Churchill was writing <em>Cloud 9</em>.</p>"},{"id":"0a194dbc-567d-4f26-944a-345899d4fb42","content":"<p>be dispassionate as they think critically about the social and political questions raised by the play.</p>"}],"keys":["0a194dbc-567d-4f26-944a-345899d4fb42"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. Churchill&rsquo;s play was influenced by Brecht&rsquo;s belief that theater should elicit an intellectual rather than an emotional response from audiences, making them think about social and political issues that also exist outside of the play. Therefore, it makes sense that Churchill would strive to have audiences think dispassionately (i.e., without emotion) and critically about the social and political questions raised by the play. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The passage mentions that Churchill was influenced by Brecht&rsquo;s belief that plays should provoke audience members &ldquo;to consider social and political realities that extend beyond the characters and events depicted onstage,&rdquo; so there&rsquo;s no basis for an inference about audience members deeply engaging with characters&rsquo; beliefs and actions. Choice B is incorrect. Reflecting on things that aren&rsquo;t related to patriarchy and colonialism wouldn&rsquo;t &ldquo;engage audiences in an interrogation of patriarchy and colonialism.&rdquo; That creates a confusing contradiction. Choice C is incorrect. No mention is made of social and political issues in Germany during Brecht&rsquo;s time, so there&rsquo;s no basis for this inference. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-INF-H005","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"b61833f1-23b8-4397-bcc1-aed2334b4260","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959368,"updateDate":1691007959368}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959368, 1691007959368),
    ($SATQ$f9c4bdab$SATQ$, $SATQ$24f0aa79-c7cc-4438-84e1-bec4e8d2a77f$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$f37d1966-4c78-4e5c-a898-909e286aefbb$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>A student is writing a paper about <em>One Night in Miami...</em>, a 2020 film directed by Regina King and written by Kemp Powers. Powers adapted the film&rsquo;s screenplay from his 2013 play, which he wrote after learning about a 1964 meeting that took place in Miami, Florida, between four prominent figures of the Civil Rights movement: Malcolm X, Muhammad Ali, Jim Brown, and Sam Cooke. The student claims that although Powers was inspired by this meeting, the film is best understood not as a precise retelling of historical events but rather as a largely imagined but informed representation of them.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which quotation from an article about <em>One Night in Miami... </em>would be the most effective evidence for the student to include in support of this claim?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"716e7c54-bc90-4155-9e31-7eae2f41d9a3","content":"<p>&ldquo;When Powers learned of the meeting, he initially planned to write a much longer work about its four famous participants rather than focusing on the meeting itself.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"5a3c5d24-f16c-46ba-ad68-a628de32461c","content":"<p>&ldquo;<em>One Night in Miami...</em> received numerous awards and nominations, including an Academy Award nomination for Powers for Best Adapted Screenplay.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"aa3cc34a-ba68-4dcd-b8ab-dd03b4930d4d","content":"<p>&ldquo;Powers has described <em>One Night in Miami... </em>as the story of four friends encouraging and supporting one another while engaged in a crucial political debate about how best to achieve equality for Black people in the United States.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"e635cbdd-690b-4d53-a1ba-d73e145754ef","content":"<p>&ldquo;Powers could find only the most superficial historical details about the meeting, so he read extensively about the four individuals and their thinking at the time in an effort to portray what might have happened between them.&rdquo;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["e635cbdd-690b-4d53-a1ba-d73e145754ef"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it provides a quotation that effectively supports the student&rsquo;s claim about the film <em>One Night in Miami&hellip;</em>. The quotation states that in researching the play on which the film was based, Kemp Powers only found superficial details about what actually happened during the 1964 meeting in Miami between four leading Civil Rights leaders, meaning that there is very little information about the meeting in the historical record. In the absence of greater details, it wouldn&rsquo;t have been possible for the film to be a precise retelling of the historical events it depicts. The quotation explains that to compensate for this lack of information about the meeting, Powers did extensive research into the four figures and how they thought at the time in order to speculate in an informed way about what they might have said or what might have occurred between them. Therefore, the quotation effectively supports the claim that the film is best understood not as a precise retelling of a historical event but as a deeply informed imaginative rendering of that event.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the quotation discusses how on learning about the 1964 meeting in Miami, Powers was inspired to write a play and, later, to adapt it into a screenplay, it doesn&rsquo;t discuss Powers&rsquo;s approach to representing what had occurred in the meeting. Instead, it states that Powers didn&rsquo;t initially plan to write a story only &ldquo;focusing on the meeting itself&rdquo; but rather had considered writing a &ldquo;much longer&rdquo; and more expansive work about the meeting&rsquo;s four participants. Choice B is incorrect because the quotation doesn&rsquo;t discuss Powers&rsquo;s approach to representing historical events in his play and in the film; instead, the quotation focuses on the film&rsquo;s positive critical reception by mentioning that it received numerous awards and nominations. Choice C is incorrect. Although the quotation references historical events that are discussed directly in the play and film by explaining how the four historical figures featured in the story engage in political debates about contemporary issues, it doesn&rsquo;t specify to what extent Powers&rsquo;s representation of what occurred during the 1964 meeting in Miami is a factual retelling of what happened and how much is an imaginative rendering of what might have happened. Rather, the quotation focuses on Powers&rsquo;s description of the film&rsquo;s basic premise and how the characters engage with the historical context of its setting. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-345-TXT$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$c7d40537-52fa-4985-af67-4fd0af1dfebe$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"f9c4bdab","external_id":"24f0aa79-c7cc-4438-84e1-bec4e8d2a77f","vaultid":"f37d1966-4c78-4e5c-a898-909e286aefbb","uId":"97e6a8ca-9753-47a1-a391-e492b88e0960","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>A student is writing a paper about <em>One Night in Miami...</em>, a 2020 film directed by Regina King and written by Kemp Powers. Powers adapted the film&rsquo;s screenplay from his 2013 play, which he wrote after learning about a 1964 meeting that took place in Miami, Florida, between four prominent figures of the Civil Rights movement: Malcolm X, Muhammad Ali, Jim Brown, and Sam Cooke. The student claims that although Powers was inspired by this meeting, the film is best understood not as a precise retelling of historical events but rather as a largely imagined but informed representation of them.</p>","stem":"<p>Which quotation from an article about <em>One Night in Miami... </em>would be the most effective evidence for the student to include in support of this claim?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"716e7c54-bc90-4155-9e31-7eae2f41d9a3","content":"<p>&ldquo;When Powers learned of the meeting, he initially planned to write a much longer work about its four famous participants rather than focusing on the meeting itself.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"5a3c5d24-f16c-46ba-ad68-a628de32461c","content":"<p>&ldquo;<em>One Night in Miami...</em> received numerous awards and nominations, including an Academy Award nomination for Powers for Best Adapted Screenplay.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"aa3cc34a-ba68-4dcd-b8ab-dd03b4930d4d","content":"<p>&ldquo;Powers has described <em>One Night in Miami... </em>as the story of four friends encouraging and supporting one another while engaged in a crucial political debate about how best to achieve equality for Black people in the United States.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"e635cbdd-690b-4d53-a1ba-d73e145754ef","content":"<p>&ldquo;Powers could find only the most superficial historical details about the meeting, so he read extensively about the four individuals and their thinking at the time in an effort to portray what might have happened between them.&rdquo;</p>"}],"keys":["e635cbdd-690b-4d53-a1ba-d73e145754ef"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it provides a quotation that effectively supports the student&rsquo;s claim about the film <em>One Night in Miami&hellip;</em>. The quotation states that in researching the play on which the film was based, Kemp Powers only found superficial details about what actually happened during the 1964 meeting in Miami between four leading Civil Rights leaders, meaning that there is very little information about the meeting in the historical record. In the absence of greater details, it wouldn&rsquo;t have been possible for the film to be a precise retelling of the historical events it depicts. The quotation explains that to compensate for this lack of information about the meeting, Powers did extensive research into the four figures and how they thought at the time in order to speculate in an informed way about what they might have said or what might have occurred between them. Therefore, the quotation effectively supports the claim that the film is best understood not as a precise retelling of a historical event but as a deeply informed imaginative rendering of that event.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the quotation discusses how on learning about the 1964 meeting in Miami, Powers was inspired to write a play and, later, to adapt it into a screenplay, it doesn&rsquo;t discuss Powers&rsquo;s approach to representing what had occurred in the meeting. Instead, it states that Powers didn&rsquo;t initially plan to write a story only &ldquo;focusing on the meeting itself&rdquo; but rather had considered writing a &ldquo;much longer&rdquo; and more expansive work about the meeting&rsquo;s four participants. Choice B is incorrect because the quotation doesn&rsquo;t discuss Powers&rsquo;s approach to representing historical events in his play and in the film; instead, the quotation focuses on the film&rsquo;s positive critical reception by mentioning that it received numerous awards and nominations. Choice C is incorrect. Although the quotation references historical events that are discussed directly in the play and film by explaining how the four historical figures featured in the story engage in political debates about contemporary issues, it doesn&rsquo;t specify to what extent Powers&rsquo;s representation of what occurred during the 1964 meeting in Miami is a factual retelling of what happened and how much is an imaginative rendering of what might have happened. Rather, the quotation focuses on Powers&rsquo;s description of the film&rsquo;s basic premise and how the characters engage with the historical context of its setting. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-345-TXT","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"c7d40537-52fa-4985-af67-4fd0af1dfebe","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959336,"updateDate":1691007959336}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959336, 1691007959336)
